00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:31.040
So where did Minigo come from?

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.320
So AlphaGo and DeepMind has
been described in three papers.

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:39.320
The first paper
describes AlphaGo,

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.010
and that is the "Mastering the
Game of Go with Deep Neural

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:46.520
Networks and Tree Search."

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:49.130
It was published in "Nature."

00:03:49.130 --> 00:03:51.830
And my friend, Brian
Lee, wrote something

00:03:51.830 --> 00:03:54.830
called MuGo or
MicroGo, which was

00:03:54.830 --> 00:03:58.550
an implementation of that
first version of the paper.

00:03:58.550 --> 00:04:00.710
He had sort of a skeleton
implementation of it.

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:04.984
It implemented one half of the
network but not the other half

00:04:04.984 --> 00:04:06.650
and was sort of like
a proof of concept.

00:04:06.650 --> 00:04:10.280
So after AlphaGo,
their second paper

00:04:10.280 --> 00:04:12.500
was called "AlphaGo Zero."

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:16.640
And AlphaGo Zero
describes an algorithm

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:21.620
that started from
nothing, from random noise

00:04:21.620 --> 00:04:24.590
and then went on to teach
itself how to play go.

00:04:24.590 --> 00:04:28.460
So mixing in AlphaGo
Zero's paper plus my friend

00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:32.720
Brian's implementation
of MuGo, we get Minigo.

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:35.420
And if you're curious
about why Minigo's logo is

00:04:35.420 --> 00:04:39.230
a happy looking robot
falling off of a ladder,

00:04:39.230 --> 00:04:40.340
all will become clear.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:43.040
I will explain why
he looks completely

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:46.130
at ease with the situation where
he's falling off of a ladder.

00:04:46.130 --> 00:04:48.740
All right, so let's talk
about what the game of Go is.

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:50.050
How many people have played Go?

00:04:50.050 --> 00:04:52.540
Anybody here-- wow
that's a lot people.

00:04:52.540 --> 00:04:54.860
How many people like to play Go?

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:56.750
Yes, that's exactly
what I like to hear.

00:04:56.750 --> 00:04:57.789
I love Go.

00:04:57.789 --> 00:04:59.330
I've been playing
Go for a long time.

00:04:59.330 --> 00:05:00.913
I really like it,
so we're going to do

00:05:00.913 --> 00:05:03.220
a quick demo of Go what is.

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:05.330
This is what it looks
like at the beginning.

00:05:05.330 --> 00:05:06.920
People take turns
putting stones down

00:05:06.920 --> 00:05:10.160
on the board trying
to surround territory.

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:12.680
When we talk about capturing,
capturing looks like this.

00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.390
When stones are
completely surrounded,

00:05:14.390 --> 00:05:16.370
they get taken off the board.

00:05:16.370 --> 00:05:18.700
That also works on
larger chains of stones,

00:05:18.700 --> 00:05:22.190
so you can see that groups
that are connected orthogonally

00:05:22.190 --> 00:05:27.740
share their fate, where they
will hang or stand together

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:29.870
as it were.

00:05:29.870 --> 00:05:31.520
The winner is decided by--

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:34.820
as you try to divide
up the board, whoever

00:05:34.820 --> 00:05:37.850
has more territory wins.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:38.350
That's it.

00:05:38.350 --> 00:05:40.669
So it's like you're
drawing up lines on a map,

00:05:40.669 --> 00:05:41.460
you're carving up--

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.360
I get this, you get that.

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:47.409
You can see on the right diagram
there that white has surrounded

00:05:47.409 --> 00:05:49.450
all the triangled points
and black has surrounded

00:05:49.450 --> 00:05:52.120
all of the squared points.

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.850
This means that Go is not really
a sort of absolutist game,

00:05:54.850 --> 00:05:56.980
where you have to
capture the enemy king

00:05:56.980 --> 00:05:59.890
or completely destroy
the enemy army.

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:02.770
It's more like you're
negotiating an agreement,

00:06:02.770 --> 00:06:05.020
where you just want to
get a little bit more

00:06:05.020 --> 00:06:06.667
than the other person.

00:06:06.667 --> 00:06:09.250
And this is an example of what
the whole game might look like.

00:06:09.250 --> 00:06:10.510
So this is Minigo in action.

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:13.270
You can see it
sketching out territory,

00:06:13.270 --> 00:06:14.344
starting in the corners.

00:06:14.344 --> 00:06:16.510
And this pattern that you
see happening right there,

00:06:16.510 --> 00:06:18.640
that is called a ladder.

00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:23.065
Ladders are an interesting
example of why Go is hard.

00:06:23.065 --> 00:06:24.940
So this is a fairly
straight forward pattern.

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:26.770
You can see it develop again.

00:06:26.770 --> 00:06:28.060
It's a really obvious pattern.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:32.510
It's a pattern that toddlers
can probably follow and predict,

00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:36.580
but it's an example where
Go has a very long horizon

00:06:36.580 --> 00:06:38.770
effect, where the
result of that ladder

00:06:38.770 --> 00:06:41.210
could decide the
outcome of the game.

00:06:41.210 --> 00:06:44.700
And it may require looking
80 to 90 moves ahead.

00:06:44.700 --> 00:06:47.650
And so why is that hard?

00:06:47.650 --> 00:06:50.290
So a ladder is
this great example

00:06:50.290 --> 00:06:52.950
of a game where you have--

00:06:52.950 --> 00:06:54.970
where even with a
branching factor of only

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.640
looking at two moves
for each of those cases,

00:06:57.640 --> 00:07:01.330
you're already looking
at or 2 to the 80th--

00:07:01.330 --> 00:07:03.010
excuse me-- possible positions.

00:07:03.010 --> 00:07:04.520
So that very high
branching factor

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:05.770
makes things really difficult.

00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.390
The games are really long.

00:07:07.390 --> 00:07:09.400
The end condition is
really hard to describe.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.250
So with Go, you want--

00:07:12.250 --> 00:07:14.650
with chess, when the king
is captured or checkmated,

00:07:14.650 --> 00:07:15.500
the game is over.

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:18.130
And everybody can see and
agree this game has ended.

00:07:18.130 --> 00:07:19.900
With Go the game
is only over when

00:07:19.900 --> 00:07:22.270
both players agree that
there's nothing left

00:07:22.270 --> 00:07:23.854
on the board worth contesting.

00:07:23.854 --> 00:07:25.270
This is pretty
difficult if you're

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:27.820
trying to teach a computer
how the game is over.

00:07:27.820 --> 00:07:31.060
In fact, just scoring
the board, just knowing

00:07:31.060 --> 00:07:35.057
that it is time to score the
board is a really hard problem.

00:07:35.057 --> 00:07:37.390
And then lastly, and this is
possibly the most important

00:07:37.390 --> 00:07:38.890
part, is that it's
really hard in Go

00:07:38.890 --> 00:07:42.670
to determine who's winning
in the middle of the game.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:45.290
This is one of the--

00:07:45.290 --> 00:07:49.031
so this problem, who's
winning, who's going to win--

00:07:49.031 --> 00:07:50.530
one of the hardest
problems we have.

00:07:50.530 --> 00:07:52.540
Dealing with the branching
factor, that there's so many

00:07:52.540 --> 00:07:55.000
possible moves at every point,
one of the really difficult

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.040
problems that we need to solve.

00:07:57.040 --> 00:07:59.080
And so let's take a
step back, and let's

00:07:59.080 --> 00:08:00.250
talk about how we're
going to approach

00:08:00.250 --> 00:08:01.375
this with machine learning.

00:08:01.375 --> 00:08:04.390
So this is five slides
about machine learning.

00:08:04.390 --> 00:08:08.244
Obviously, this is not an
exhaustive explanation.

00:08:08.244 --> 00:08:09.910
I should mention
also-- this is probably

00:08:09.910 --> 00:08:12.979
a good time-- none of us
have PhDs on the Minigo team.

00:08:12.979 --> 00:08:14.770
So there's probably
people in this audience

00:08:14.770 --> 00:08:18.790
who understand this better
than I do, but bear with me.

00:08:18.790 --> 00:08:20.950
I hope that this will be
enlightening for folks

00:08:20.950 --> 00:08:23.560
who have maybe not done any
machine learning before at all.

00:08:23.560 --> 00:08:26.120
So real quick-- inference
for neural networks.

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.170
So the basic idea is we're
going to put in an input

00:08:29.170 --> 00:08:32.440
and get out an output, and
that thing in the middle

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.667
is the model that we talk about.

00:08:34.667 --> 00:08:36.250
We don't really want
to worry too much

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:40.003
about what it is, except
for we need to know

00:08:40.003 --> 00:08:41.169
a couple of things about it.

00:08:41.169 --> 00:08:42.940
The first is that
it's a bunch of math

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:46.390
that is differentiable or
close enough to differentiable.

00:08:46.390 --> 00:08:48.760
And the second is that
it's really slow--

00:08:48.760 --> 00:08:51.760
like slow on the order of
milliseconds to evaluate.

00:08:51.760 --> 00:08:54.140
And why does that matter
that it takes milliseconds?

00:08:54.140 --> 00:08:55.222
It seems pretty fast.

00:08:55.222 --> 00:08:57.430
Well, because you maybe need
to do thousands of those

00:08:57.430 --> 00:09:00.190
before you can decide on
a move that you can play.

00:09:00.190 --> 00:09:02.860
So we're going to consider
neural networks for inference,

00:09:02.860 --> 00:09:04.810
inference meaning
this forward path,

00:09:04.810 --> 00:09:09.820
where we start at our input
and we get out our decision.

00:09:09.820 --> 00:09:13.090
That's what inference is, it's
also called a forward pass.

00:09:13.090 --> 00:09:16.100
And we need to know that it's
slow and it's differentiable.

00:09:16.100 --> 00:09:19.840
All right, so how do
we create that model,

00:09:19.840 --> 00:09:21.100
that thing in the middle?

00:09:21.100 --> 00:09:23.724
Well, what we're going to do is
we're going to try and quantify

00:09:23.724 --> 00:09:27.070
the error, where we have
inputs that we know and outputs

00:09:27.070 --> 00:09:28.807
that we should have gotten.

00:09:28.807 --> 00:09:31.390
And we're going to take a look
at the difference between those

00:09:31.390 --> 00:09:34.444
and try and change the model,
which is differentiable,

00:09:34.444 --> 00:09:35.860
so we know which
direction we need

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.860
to push the different
values in that model

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.267
to make the error smaller.

00:09:40.267 --> 00:09:41.850
So that's, basically,
machine learning

00:09:41.850 --> 00:09:44.470
in a nutshell-- at least, the
stochastic gradient descent

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.670
form of machine learning.

00:09:46.670 --> 00:09:48.370
But yeah, it's a pile
of linear algebra,

00:09:48.370 --> 00:09:50.899
and we're going to
try to tweak it.

00:09:50.899 --> 00:09:52.690
And we can repeat this
process where we try

00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.270
and make the error slightly
smaller until we run out

00:09:55.270 --> 00:09:57.130
of data.

00:09:57.130 --> 00:09:59.080
That function, that
model in the middle

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.037
could be a function of many,
many millions of variables.

00:10:02.037 --> 00:10:04.120
So we're, essentially,
trying to minimize the loss

00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:07.939
of million variable functions.

00:10:07.939 --> 00:10:09.730
And that's pretty
complicated, but luckily,

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:11.146
there are pretty
good abstractions

00:10:11.146 --> 00:10:12.857
for doing all this stuff.

00:10:12.857 --> 00:10:13.690
So let's keep going.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:15.731
So what does inference
for Minigo, and how are we

00:10:15.731 --> 00:10:17.570
going to use that to
solve this problem?

00:10:17.570 --> 00:10:22.210
So inference for Minigo, in
this case, means two questions.

00:10:22.210 --> 00:10:24.980
What moves should we play, who
do we think is going to win?

00:10:24.980 --> 00:10:28.105
So given that board that you see
on the left, that's our input,

00:10:28.105 --> 00:10:31.240
and we're going to ask
Minigo go for a probability

00:10:31.240 --> 00:10:36.350
distribution of where it thinks
the next move is going to be.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:40.000
And we're also going to ask
it for a number that expresses

00:10:40.000 --> 00:10:41.780
who it thinks is going to win.

00:10:41.780 --> 00:10:44.350
So Minigo uses negative 1
for if white is going to win,

00:10:44.350 --> 00:10:46.840
and positive 1 if
black is going to win.

00:10:46.840 --> 00:10:50.230
Zero says way too close to
call-- perfectly balanced--

00:10:50.230 --> 00:10:52.810
and anywhere in there
from negative 1 to 1

00:10:52.810 --> 00:10:56.900
is it quantifying who it
thinks is going to win.

00:10:56.900 --> 00:11:01.420
So given those two
outputs, what is

00:11:01.420 --> 00:11:04.210
the move that we think
is going to be played,

00:11:04.210 --> 00:11:06.160
who do we think is
going to win, we

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:09.640
do something called
Monte Carlo tree search.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:12.130
My friend Brian Lee, who
I mentioned wrote MuGo,

00:11:12.130 --> 00:11:15.940
did a great talk at Pycon 2018
called "A Deep Dive into Monte

00:11:15.940 --> 00:11:18.310
Carlo Tree Search" with code.

00:11:18.310 --> 00:11:21.830
Also our code is on GitHub,
the Monte Carlo tree

00:11:21.830 --> 00:11:24.580
search is really great,
super easy to read,

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:26.050
highly recommended.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:31.270
So that deep dive into Monte
Carlo tree search is great.

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.660
So by doing this self-play
with Monte Carlo tree search,

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:40.030
we're able to
quantify our error.

00:11:40.030 --> 00:11:43.570
So we asked Minigo for what
move to play and who was ahead.

00:11:43.570 --> 00:11:46.240
And now, we can define
our error as the sum

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:49.870
of the difference between
the original estimate

00:11:49.870 --> 00:11:52.960
for what move to play and
the moves that were actually

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:54.520
explored by tree search.

00:11:54.520 --> 00:11:56.350
So we use that
estimate of who's going

00:11:56.350 --> 00:11:58.930
to win to decide whether we
continue to explore that move.

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:00.700
So we'll look at
this move, then we'll

00:12:00.700 --> 00:12:02.740
look at the next most
likely one under it.

00:12:02.740 --> 00:12:04.660
And if it's better
or worse, maybe, we

00:12:04.660 --> 00:12:06.400
won't explore that parent move.

00:12:06.400 --> 00:12:08.417
Does that make sense,
kind of, maybe?

00:12:08.417 --> 00:12:10.000
Can we can we just
sort of acknowledge

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:11.770
that this could
happen, and we can

00:12:11.770 --> 00:12:14.045
dig into the details
at some later point.

00:12:14.045 --> 00:12:16.420
All right, so that's Monte
Carlo tree search-- which move

00:12:16.420 --> 00:12:17.086
and who's ahead.

00:12:17.086 --> 00:12:19.360
The two components-- which
move and who's ahead--

00:12:19.360 --> 00:12:21.740
are called the
policy and the value

00:12:21.740 --> 00:12:22.990
outputs of the neural network.

00:12:22.990 --> 00:12:25.990
So policy is given
a board state,

00:12:25.990 --> 00:12:28.120
where do I think the
next play is going to be?

00:12:28.120 --> 00:12:32.620
And value is how do
I value this board.

00:12:32.620 --> 00:12:35.620
All right, so let's
do the quick recap,

00:12:35.620 --> 00:12:36.770
that was our fifth slide.

00:12:36.770 --> 00:12:39.810
So this is our summary slide,
this is how I get six slides.

00:12:39.810 --> 00:12:41.560
This is our reinforcement
learning recap--

00:12:41.560 --> 00:12:44.350
the lightning version of
reinforcement learning--

00:12:44.350 --> 00:12:48.640
is using our data
to make models,

00:12:48.640 --> 00:12:51.186
using our models then
to make more data,

00:12:51.186 --> 00:12:52.560
and now we never
run out of data,

00:12:52.560 --> 00:12:54.320
and we can keep doing
this as much as we want.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:55.153
So what's the model?

00:12:55.153 --> 00:12:58.000
The model is that pile of math
that we had in the middle.

00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:00.550
We want to be able to measure
that loss by comparing it

00:13:00.550 --> 00:13:03.100
with answers that we
know or with answers

00:13:03.100 --> 00:13:04.570
that we think we know.

00:13:04.570 --> 00:13:08.110
We train a model by
minimizing that error.

00:13:08.110 --> 00:13:10.900
And Minigo uses the
policy and value

00:13:10.900 --> 00:13:15.130
to do tree search to try and
refine its original estimates.

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:17.140
Does that make sense?

00:13:17.140 --> 00:13:20.540
Maybe-- somebody, yeah, no?

00:13:20.540 --> 00:13:22.930
OK, well, we're just
going to move on.

00:13:22.930 --> 00:13:24.420
The reinforcement
learning loop--

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:25.040
pretty simple.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:27.180
It's this nice virtuous
cycle that we can set up,

00:13:27.180 --> 00:13:29.820
where self-play
makes better data,

00:13:29.820 --> 00:13:32.520
and we use that in training
to make better models.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:33.940
And they reinforce each other.

00:13:33.940 --> 00:13:35.350
The problem is that--

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:39.150
I've made one of those
errors substantially larger,

00:13:39.150 --> 00:13:41.940
and that's not an
accident, because the idea

00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:44.730
is that doing tree
search involves

00:13:44.730 --> 00:13:48.510
doing many of these
inferences or forward passes.

00:13:48.510 --> 00:13:50.520
We want to do as
many readouts as we

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:54.520
need to be able to ensure that
we're improving our policy.

00:13:54.520 --> 00:13:57.450
And that means doing maybe
hundreds or thousands

00:13:57.450 --> 00:14:00.394
of inferences per each move.

00:14:00.394 --> 00:14:01.810
Now, if we can
train on each move,

00:14:01.810 --> 00:14:04.260
that means we're doing hundreds
or thousands of inferences

00:14:04.260 --> 00:14:07.000
per training data
point that we make.

00:14:07.000 --> 00:14:10.890
So at a minimum, we're going
to have this ratio where

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:14.490
we need to do hundreds to one
in terms of making inferences

00:14:14.490 --> 00:14:16.890
versus making training steps.

00:14:16.890 --> 00:14:19.870
Does that sort of set
up the problem here?

00:14:19.870 --> 00:14:22.260
So in order to
train adequately, I

00:14:22.260 --> 00:14:24.210
need to do this for
millions of games,

00:14:24.210 --> 00:14:27.570
and I know that a game will
take on the order of minutes

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:29.770
even when I'm
using GPUs or TPUs.

00:14:29.770 --> 00:14:34.230
So my question becomes,
how do I scale this?

00:14:34.230 --> 00:14:36.760
Now, happily this is
a problem that is--

00:14:36.760 --> 00:14:39.090
to borrow someone's phrase--
embarrassingly parallel.

00:14:41.790 --> 00:14:43.620
Having two copies of
Minigo play each other

00:14:43.620 --> 00:14:45.930
does not involve
any other copies

00:14:45.930 --> 00:14:48.780
of Minigo, which means that I
can spin up as many of these

00:14:48.780 --> 00:14:53.400
as I can, assuming I have a
good way to shard this all out

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:54.600
or scale this all out.

00:14:54.600 --> 00:14:56.647
And so that brings us
to the part of the talk

00:14:56.647 --> 00:14:58.230
that I think we're
all here for, which

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.630
is using this on Google Cloud.

00:15:00.630 --> 00:15:04.650
So we used Kubernetes
to do this,

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:08.417
but we started by making the
smallest possible units of work

00:15:08.417 --> 00:15:09.000
that we could.

00:15:09.000 --> 00:15:11.590
So our training job was
pretty straightforward.

00:15:11.590 --> 00:15:14.280
And we made our worker
job use Cloud storage

00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:17.730
as a way to sort of coordinate
what we needed to do.

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:19.830
This worked really well.

00:15:19.830 --> 00:15:23.850
Cloud Storage, as it turns
out, is incredibly flexible,

00:15:23.850 --> 00:15:27.970
and you can abuse it in all
sorts of interesting ways.

00:15:27.970 --> 00:15:30.420
We were able to use
Cloud Storage to get

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:32.940
the sort of tracer bullet,
while we're sort of deciding

00:15:32.940 --> 00:15:34.650
do we need to write
a centralized server

00:15:34.650 --> 00:15:37.140
or do these need to
talk to a server at all?

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.300
And Cloud Storage, basically,
scaled to the point

00:15:39.300 --> 00:15:40.620
that we didn't really bother.

00:15:40.620 --> 00:15:44.370
So it stood up
wonderfully, and we've

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:47.910
been able to use that
probably beyond the limits

00:15:47.910 --> 00:15:49.530
that we certainly
thought we could.

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.300
Then what we did was we
turned this into containers.

00:15:52.300 --> 00:15:55.710
So we used Docker
to build containers

00:15:55.710 --> 00:16:01.410
for that self-pay worker, and
we used Kubernetes to scale this

00:16:01.410 --> 00:16:04.104
out to thousands of nodes.

00:16:04.104 --> 00:16:05.520
So we did this,
we could start up,

00:16:05.520 --> 00:16:06.780
we could fetch the latest model.

00:16:06.780 --> 00:16:07.950
We could write out our data.

00:16:07.950 --> 00:16:10.140
We could shut down
and start up again.

00:16:10.140 --> 00:16:13.110
And since each time, we're
fetching the latest version

00:16:13.110 --> 00:16:16.860
of the model, we're always able
to coordinate and make sure

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:19.530
that we're generating new data
with the latest and best model

00:16:19.530 --> 00:16:22.310
that we have.

00:16:22.310 --> 00:16:24.790
So what we originally
did was we originally

00:16:24.790 --> 00:16:26.540
wrote this on a
smaller board size.

00:16:26.540 --> 00:16:29.650
So the original diagrams that
I showed you at the beginning

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:32.800
were Go being played
on a 19 by 19 board--

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.510
that is the full
size game of Go.

00:16:34.510 --> 00:16:36.760
Go is also played
on a 9 by 9 board.

00:16:36.760 --> 00:16:40.630
And this 9 by 9 board
is a lot simpler.

00:16:40.630 --> 00:16:44.840
The model for that is
about 250 times faster.

00:16:44.840 --> 00:16:46.540
So we were able
to sort of verify

00:16:46.540 --> 00:16:49.900
correctness using that smaller
model on a smaller Go board.

00:16:49.900 --> 00:16:52.360
And I'm using
correctness very loosely.

00:16:52.360 --> 00:16:54.940
Because, as it turns
out, machine learning

00:16:54.940 --> 00:16:59.387
is very good at finding
patterns in your data or--

00:16:59.387 --> 00:17:01.720
patterns in your data that
you didn't mean to put there.

00:17:01.720 --> 00:17:04.790
It's very good at covering up
for mistakes that you make.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:08.740
So we saw something get better
on our smaller sized board that

00:17:08.740 --> 00:17:12.250
had really glaring,
horrible bugs

00:17:12.250 --> 00:17:15.190
that we wouldn't discover
until later but more on that.

00:17:15.190 --> 00:17:18.028
The next thing we did-- so
after we did this and verified

00:17:18.028 --> 00:17:19.569
our correctness on
the smaller board,

00:17:19.569 --> 00:17:22.210
we realized that, holy
cow, the full size model

00:17:22.210 --> 00:17:24.069
is going to be 250 times slower.

00:17:24.069 --> 00:17:26.020
We need to add accelerators.

00:17:26.020 --> 00:17:30.370
And so we were able to hook up
GPUs with a minimum of fuss.

00:17:30.370 --> 00:17:32.890
We were running a cluster
of about 2,100 GPUs

00:17:32.890 --> 00:17:37.060
units with no problem at all.

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:39.250
Using GPUs on
Kubernetes is fantastic.

00:17:39.250 --> 00:17:41.634
It means we don't have
to worry about drivers.

00:17:41.634 --> 00:17:43.300
We didn't have to
make any code changes.

00:17:43.300 --> 00:17:45.520
What ran on our workstation
would run perfectly

00:17:45.520 --> 00:17:51.010
on Kubernetes Engine.

00:17:51.010 --> 00:17:53.590
And this was a
pretty great success,

00:17:53.590 --> 00:17:55.310
but it was still a little slow.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:57.146
We had some benchmark
performance numbers

00:17:57.146 --> 00:17:58.270
that we were trying to hit.

00:17:58.270 --> 00:18:02.200
So the numbers in
the AlphaGo paper

00:18:02.200 --> 00:18:06.070
detailed that they were able to
perform 1,600 inferences in 0.4

00:18:06.070 --> 00:18:07.510
seconds.

00:18:07.510 --> 00:18:10.990
We were about 40 times
slower than that,

00:18:10.990 --> 00:18:13.390
which is OK for a
solution that was Python,

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:17.650
and for our accelerators,
which were not TPUs.

00:18:17.650 --> 00:18:21.070
But 40 times slower
meant 40 times slower.

00:18:21.070 --> 00:18:25.944
They were able to train, play
5 million games in three days.

00:18:25.944 --> 00:18:27.610
And if we are 40 times
slower than that,

00:18:27.610 --> 00:18:31.310
I'm looking at this
taking three months.

00:18:31.310 --> 00:18:32.800
So that's a little challenging.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.174
I needed to find a way
to do this more faster.

00:18:35.174 --> 00:18:37.090
So before we do that,
let's take a little step

00:18:37.090 --> 00:18:39.252
and say that as I'm
setting this up on GPUs

00:18:39.252 --> 00:18:41.710
and I'm realizing that this is
going to take months to run,

00:18:41.710 --> 00:18:43.630
I really wanted to
find ways to verify

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:45.130
that everything was working.

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:46.810
So with containers,
it was really easy

00:18:46.810 --> 00:18:49.810
for me to make variations
on the jobs I was running

00:18:49.810 --> 00:18:54.272
and really try to run other
sorts of evaluation matches.

00:18:54.272 --> 00:18:56.230
It was really easy to
use the Kubernetes Engine

00:18:56.230 --> 00:18:59.080
API to spin up jobs
that were variants,

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:01.000
so I could test different
version of models--

00:19:01.000 --> 00:19:03.386
make sure that I'm
making progress.

00:19:03.386 --> 00:19:04.760
This was a really
important thing

00:19:04.760 --> 00:19:06.040
that I'm going to
come back to later.

00:19:06.040 --> 00:19:07.623
And when I say,
measuring performance,

00:19:07.623 --> 00:19:09.340
I'm measuring
performance on my task,

00:19:09.340 --> 00:19:13.450
which is, are my models actually
getting better at playing Go?

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:15.140
That's a real question.

00:19:15.140 --> 00:19:20.110
So early on, I knew that this
was going to take three months.

00:19:20.110 --> 00:19:24.410
At the point that Cloud TPUs
were being developed I said,

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.440
hey, this might work
really well if I could

00:19:26.440 --> 00:19:28.450
try running this on Cloud TPUs.

00:19:28.450 --> 00:19:30.460
And the Cloud TPU team
was pretty enthusiastic,

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:33.110
and they said, yes,
sure, go right ahead.

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.380
But they were so
much faster that it

00:19:35.380 --> 00:19:38.350
meant really reevaluating
how I had originally

00:19:38.350 --> 00:19:40.360
written my pipeline.

00:19:40.360 --> 00:19:42.610
For reference, the 2,000
GPUs that I was using

00:19:42.610 --> 00:19:45.070
were a few generations
old, so I'm deliberately

00:19:45.070 --> 00:19:49.420
avoiding making any sort of
direct numerical comparison.

00:19:49.420 --> 00:19:54.130
But suffice to say that what
was previously fine in Python

00:19:54.130 --> 00:19:57.220
was now no longer fine
using a Cloud TPU.

00:19:57.220 --> 00:19:58.750
There was no way
that the code was

00:19:58.750 --> 00:20:00.949
going to be fast
enough, which meant

00:20:00.949 --> 00:20:03.490
that if I was going to be able
to use these TPUs effectively,

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:07.060
I would need to seriously
rethink how this pipeline was

00:20:07.060 --> 00:20:08.141
going to run.

00:20:08.141 --> 00:20:10.390
When you're planning on
something taking three months,

00:20:10.390 --> 00:20:12.820
and now you're looking
at maybe a week or two,

00:20:12.820 --> 00:20:14.770
you have some very
different constraints

00:20:14.770 --> 00:20:17.890
about how long you can take
to preprocess your input data,

00:20:17.890 --> 00:20:22.390
how long you can take
to lazily push out

00:20:22.390 --> 00:20:24.024
the results of the new models.

00:20:24.024 --> 00:20:25.190
It's all that sort of thing.

00:20:25.190 --> 00:20:29.140
So what we had to do was
we had to rewrite for TPUs.

00:20:29.140 --> 00:20:31.390
This code is Monte
Carlo tree search.

00:20:31.390 --> 00:20:34.192
This is the short pseudocode
for Monte Carlo tree search.

00:20:34.192 --> 00:20:36.400
And I'd like to draw your
attention to that line that

00:20:36.400 --> 00:20:37.795
says
NeuralNet.evaluate(leaf.game

00:20:37.795 --> 00:20:43.720
state), because that is the one
that suddenly this whole thing

00:20:43.720 --> 00:20:46.660
needed to go in parallel.

00:20:46.660 --> 00:20:50.860
And it needed to go in parallel
a lot faster than it could.

00:20:50.860 --> 00:20:53.020
And so this was the part--

00:20:53.020 --> 00:20:55.780
the engine that would do
this rapidly was the part

00:20:55.780 --> 00:20:57.400
that we needed to rewrite.

00:20:57.400 --> 00:21:00.400
So that's what it looks like
on a single threaded version.

00:21:00.400 --> 00:21:03.010
This is pretty close to what
the Python code actually

00:21:03.010 --> 00:21:07.810
looks like in Minigo today.

00:21:07.810 --> 00:21:12.220
And so rewriting it for TPUs
involved breaking this out

00:21:12.220 --> 00:21:14.960
into a multi-threaded version.

00:21:14.960 --> 00:21:20.620
A friend of mine volunteered
to do the C++ rewrite, and--

00:21:20.620 --> 00:21:23.070
he's probably not going
like me telling his story.

00:21:23.070 --> 00:21:25.560
But he was able to write
this complete multithreaded

00:21:25.560 --> 00:21:27.150
implementation--

00:21:27.150 --> 00:21:31.190
no problem, no bugs,
doing a full rewrite,

00:21:31.190 --> 00:21:34.500
multithreaded code, no
bugs, no race conditions,

00:21:34.500 --> 00:21:36.010
no problems like that.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:38.340
But he had forgotten to
actually increment a loop

00:21:38.340 --> 00:21:41.974
and was left with a brutal
bug that took days to debug.

00:21:41.974 --> 00:21:43.140
He was like, oh, I'm so bad.

00:21:43.140 --> 00:21:45.120
I'm like, you just
did a complete rewrite

00:21:45.120 --> 00:21:47.489
of multithreaded code that
had no bugs the first time.

00:21:47.489 --> 00:21:49.530
And you're going to be
hard on yourself for that.

00:21:49.530 --> 00:21:51.330
Anyway, so this is
what it looked like.

00:21:51.330 --> 00:21:55.020
We ended up building a container
out of our C++ port that would

00:21:55.020 --> 00:21:58.590
spin up a Python engine, and we
would do these RPC calls when

00:21:58.590 --> 00:22:01.510
our queue had enough nodes
in it to run them all through

00:22:01.510 --> 00:22:02.190
the model.

00:22:02.190 --> 00:22:03.197
And this did great--

00:22:03.197 --> 00:22:05.280
it also meant that our C++
code no longer depended

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:08.100
on TensorFlow, so it didn't have
to have any trouble building

00:22:08.100 --> 00:22:12.060
TensorFlow, which made
our builds a lot quicker,

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:13.842
which is pretty cool.

00:22:13.842 --> 00:22:15.300
And once we had
this engine, now we

00:22:15.300 --> 00:22:17.310
could change to
using deployments

00:22:17.310 --> 00:22:18.600
on Kubernetes Engine.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:21.752
Previously, when we were using
GPUs, we used the batch API.

00:22:21.752 --> 00:22:23.460
Quick show of hands,
people who have used

00:22:23.460 --> 00:22:25.176
Kubernetes before in this room?

00:22:25.176 --> 00:22:27.040
Cool, that's great.

00:22:27.040 --> 00:22:29.190
The batch API
worked really well.

00:22:29.190 --> 00:22:33.000
With 2,000 nodes, we had
some interesting limits

00:22:33.000 --> 00:22:35.176
around tracking completions.

00:22:35.176 --> 00:22:37.050
And what we ended up
doing was not bothering.

00:22:37.050 --> 00:22:42.900
We ended up writing our job as
a job where we set something

00:22:42.900 --> 00:22:44.364
like 100,000 completions.

00:22:44.364 --> 00:22:45.780
And what that would
actually do is

00:22:45.780 --> 00:22:48.480
it would throw away
completions after about 1,000,

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:50.680
which meant that it would
just run constantly,

00:22:50.680 --> 00:22:52.230
which was what we wanted.

00:22:52.230 --> 00:22:55.440
The completions--
writing it as a batch API

00:22:55.440 --> 00:22:59.010
worked really well, because that
meant that it would just always

00:22:59.010 --> 00:22:59.580
retry.

00:22:59.580 --> 00:23:03.690
It would handle the
retry logic for us.

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:04.870
That all worked really well.

00:23:04.870 --> 00:23:06.690
But now with Cloud
TPUs, we didn't

00:23:06.690 --> 00:23:12.150
want to pay the price of
having the containers go down

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:13.170
and come up again.

00:23:13.170 --> 00:23:17.350
So we wrote them to be long
running jobs as deployments.

00:23:17.350 --> 00:23:18.837
And this all worked really well.

00:23:18.837 --> 00:23:20.670
We didn't need to track
completions anymore,

00:23:20.670 --> 00:23:23.170
because we were writing
these as long running jobs.

00:23:23.170 --> 00:23:28.620
And so this all just, basically,
worked out of the box.

00:23:28.620 --> 00:23:31.890
One of the advantages
of Minigo is

00:23:31.890 --> 00:23:35.400
that we were able to test this.

00:23:35.400 --> 00:23:36.720
So Minigo is entirely public.

00:23:36.720 --> 00:23:38.490
It's all available
on GitHub, which

00:23:38.490 --> 00:23:40.890
meant that throughout
this entire project,

00:23:40.890 --> 00:23:43.380
I was following
the exact same path

00:23:43.380 --> 00:23:46.470
that external
customers would follow.

00:23:46.470 --> 00:23:48.150
Although, I'm a
Googler, I, basically,

00:23:48.150 --> 00:23:50.070
was going through the
exact same process

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:51.571
that anybody in
this room would have

00:23:51.571 --> 00:23:52.861
if they tried to go through it.

00:23:52.861 --> 00:23:54.690
And that was a pretty
great experience.

00:23:54.690 --> 00:23:57.060
Honestly, I hit way fewer--

00:23:57.060 --> 00:24:00.540
and also a lot of these
were while these products

00:24:00.540 --> 00:24:03.600
were pre-alpha or
getting ready for alpha.

00:24:03.600 --> 00:24:06.470
And the experience
was honestly amazing.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:09.390
Cloud TPUs on Kubernetes
Engine have basically

00:24:09.390 --> 00:24:10.950
worked out of the box.

00:24:10.950 --> 00:24:13.440
Ditto for the GPUs, like I
said, the driver problems

00:24:13.440 --> 00:24:15.990
were not even a thing
that I had to worry about.

00:24:15.990 --> 00:24:19.680
So if you're thinking about
doing ML or accelerated

00:24:19.680 --> 00:24:22.890
computing on Kubernetes
Engine, I, basically, only

00:24:22.890 --> 00:24:25.712
have good things
to say about it.

00:24:25.712 --> 00:24:26.670
So that's pretty great.

00:24:26.670 --> 00:24:29.850
So let me do a quick
demo of Minigo.

00:24:29.850 --> 00:24:31.920
I think there's a way
for me to make this play,

00:24:31.920 --> 00:24:33.672
but I don't know what it is.

00:24:33.672 --> 00:24:35.095
There we go.

00:24:35.095 --> 00:24:36.500
That looks like it.

00:24:36.500 --> 00:24:40.179
So this Minigo-- thinking about
this particular game, this

00:24:40.179 --> 00:24:41.220
is that small size board.

00:24:41.220 --> 00:24:43.880
I think this was actually
running in CPU only

00:24:43.880 --> 00:24:45.005
on a laptop.

00:24:45.005 --> 00:24:46.942
It may even have
been a Chromebook.

00:24:46.942 --> 00:24:49.400
So this is running completely
unaccelerated on that smaller

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:53.280
size of Go that I was
telling you about-- oops.

00:24:53.280 --> 00:24:56.170
And it doesn't loop,
which is unfortunate.

00:24:56.170 --> 00:24:58.050
But you can see the
variations that it's

00:24:58.050 --> 00:24:59.736
considering in the
upper right, what

00:24:59.736 --> 00:25:01.110
it thinks is the
most likely move

00:25:01.110 --> 00:25:02.687
to happen in the left side.

00:25:02.687 --> 00:25:04.020
And this is a pretty great tool.

00:25:04.020 --> 00:25:05.070
If you're a Go
player, you can really

00:25:05.070 --> 00:25:06.736
dig in and see what
it's thinking about,

00:25:06.736 --> 00:25:09.690
why, what it likes,
what it doesn't like--

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:10.950
pretty great.

00:25:10.950 --> 00:25:15.030
All right, so talking
about our results--

00:25:15.030 --> 00:25:18.660
All right, so results, OK, so
talking about the conclusions

00:25:18.660 --> 00:25:22.409
and the lessons learned, I kind
of want to talk a little bit

00:25:22.409 --> 00:25:23.950
about the trends
here that we've had,

00:25:23.950 --> 00:25:28.020
which is moving from GPUs
to TPUs-- and, in this case,

00:25:28.020 --> 00:25:32.670
these were older GPUs
to the newest TPUs--

00:25:32.670 --> 00:25:34.920
has been eye-opening.

00:25:34.920 --> 00:25:37.950
The ability for
us to do more work

00:25:37.950 --> 00:25:40.500
with basically the same
amount of electricity

00:25:40.500 --> 00:25:44.160
means that us users have
our incentives aligned

00:25:44.160 --> 00:25:48.840
with Cloud providers to use the
newest and fastest hardware.

00:25:48.840 --> 00:25:51.540
Basically, there's
no reason that we

00:25:51.540 --> 00:25:54.330
want people to use the older
versions of the hardware,

00:25:54.330 --> 00:25:57.280
because the newer
versions of the hardware

00:25:57.280 --> 00:26:00.537
will do more work with
the same wattage, which

00:26:00.537 --> 00:26:03.120
means that we really want you
to use the newest thing that you

00:26:03.120 --> 00:26:04.140
can.

00:26:04.140 --> 00:26:06.480
So as long as this
holds, there's

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:08.880
going to be this
pressure for us to make

00:26:08.880 --> 00:26:11.930
our code go as fast as
possible, and this is fantastic.

00:26:11.930 --> 00:26:13.680
In particular, when
you're doing research,

00:26:13.680 --> 00:26:16.290
if you're trying to
dial in these solutions,

00:26:16.290 --> 00:26:18.000
you need to be
able to experiment

00:26:18.000 --> 00:26:19.860
as quickly as you can.

00:26:19.860 --> 00:26:22.110
You need to be able to try
things that might have been

00:26:22.110 --> 00:26:25.110
too expensive to try otherwise.

00:26:25.110 --> 00:26:29.370
And so having this push to use
the best price per performance

00:26:29.370 --> 00:26:32.860
and having the price
per performance

00:26:32.860 --> 00:26:35.830
use the fastest thing,
not use the slower thing,

00:26:35.830 --> 00:26:38.980
means that we can really
dial in the models

00:26:38.980 --> 00:26:42.885
that we're trying to
build, that we can really

00:26:42.885 --> 00:26:44.860
iterate faster on
how we're going

00:26:44.860 --> 00:26:47.686
to test the various ideas we
have for how to develop models.

00:26:47.686 --> 00:26:49.060
But what that
means is that means

00:26:49.060 --> 00:26:51.730
you should plan for your
pipeline to get faster.

00:26:51.730 --> 00:26:55.900
Again, we had originally
bracketed that this would

00:26:55.900 --> 00:26:57.315
take us a couple of months.

00:26:57.315 --> 00:26:58.690
And now, we're
looking at getting

00:26:58.690 --> 00:27:00.650
it done in a week or two.

00:27:00.650 --> 00:27:03.940
And that changed some of our
core assumptions about how long

00:27:03.940 --> 00:27:05.972
we'd have to do a lot
of the different steps

00:27:05.972 --> 00:27:07.930
in that pipeline, and it
seems like that's only

00:27:07.930 --> 00:27:08.679
going to continue.

00:27:08.679 --> 00:27:11.140
So definitely you want
to plan accordingly.

00:27:11.140 --> 00:27:15.430
That is the Cloud Edge TPU,
which is the little UBS gum

00:27:15.430 --> 00:27:19.017
stick on which we're going to
make Minigo, which I'm really

00:27:19.017 --> 00:27:19.600
excited about.

00:27:22.240 --> 00:27:24.730
In relation to this idea
that your pipeline is going

00:27:24.730 --> 00:27:28.000
to be faster, we really
want to try and make

00:27:28.000 --> 00:27:31.090
our models more like
cattle and less like pets.

00:27:31.090 --> 00:27:34.675
You guys with the cattle not
pets metaphor from containers?

00:27:34.675 --> 00:27:36.550
Some folks may be getting
the idea-- the idea

00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:41.410
being that containers lets
you reproduce the exact build

00:27:41.410 --> 00:27:42.940
of the software you're running.

00:27:42.940 --> 00:27:45.790
You can say that my
server is interchangeable,

00:27:45.790 --> 00:27:46.990
that I can get more of them.

00:27:46.990 --> 00:27:49.480
I don't need to give it
a name, it's not special.

00:27:49.480 --> 00:27:53.410
It's not a big deal if it
dies or goes down or breaks,

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:55.570
and I have to rebuild it.

00:27:55.570 --> 00:27:57.820
In the same way, we'd like
our neural network models

00:27:57.820 --> 00:28:00.280
to be like that as well,
where if it takes you

00:28:00.280 --> 00:28:02.980
three months to do it, you
get kind of possessive.

00:28:02.980 --> 00:28:06.050
Like, you really don't want
to see anything happen to it.

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:08.440
But if you can make a
new one in a day or two,

00:28:08.440 --> 00:28:11.590
now, you have this freedom and
ability to experiment and try

00:28:11.590 --> 00:28:13.210
things.

00:28:13.210 --> 00:28:15.130
And it means that you
can keep those changes

00:28:15.130 --> 00:28:16.690
much better isolated.

00:28:16.690 --> 00:28:20.020
With our early runs on GPUs
that were taking months,

00:28:20.020 --> 00:28:22.300
it was very tempting
to constantly monitor

00:28:22.300 --> 00:28:24.760
the dashboard, try
and tweak some knobs,

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:26.920
try and turn some
dials here and there,

00:28:26.920 --> 00:28:28.450
and worry and fret over it.

00:28:28.450 --> 00:28:30.850
But it also meant that at
the end of that process,

00:28:30.850 --> 00:28:33.490
how could I reproduce exactly
that sequence of things

00:28:33.490 --> 00:28:34.330
that happened?

00:28:34.330 --> 00:28:37.510
Really hard-- having
it all go faster

00:28:37.510 --> 00:28:39.700
means that it's all a
lot more repeatable,

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.100
and that's pretty great.

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:43.930
Another thing that's
pretty interesting

00:28:43.930 --> 00:28:46.540
here is that these reinforcement
learning systems really

00:28:46.540 --> 00:28:48.050
resemble distributed systems.

00:28:48.050 --> 00:28:50.920
So I imagine a lot of folks
here work in the cloud,

00:28:50.920 --> 00:28:52.990
and they're familiar
with the idea

00:28:52.990 --> 00:28:56.590
that your individually,
well-behaved systems stop

00:28:56.590 --> 00:28:59.620
being well-behaved in tandem.

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:03.490
And that's pretty interesting
from a number of reasons.

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:05.410
I think that there's
sort of two ways

00:29:05.410 --> 00:29:08.380
that we go about analyzing
why our systems are

00:29:08.380 --> 00:29:09.610
doing the wrong thing.

00:29:09.610 --> 00:29:14.230
One of them is from the code up,
where we can look at the code

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:16.600
and understand exactly
what will happen.

00:29:16.600 --> 00:29:19.270
And the other way is
from the behaviors

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:23.740
that we see down, where we have
to think about what's happening

00:29:23.740 --> 00:29:26.290
and generate a hypothesis.

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:29.750
And say, OK, if the load
balancer is doing this,

00:29:29.750 --> 00:29:32.641
then we would expect this,
and I can test that this way.

00:29:32.641 --> 00:29:34.390
Maybe you can't solve
that just by looking

00:29:34.390 --> 00:29:36.730
at the load balancers
code and the servers--

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:39.160
you have to generate a
hypothesis and test it.

00:29:39.160 --> 00:29:41.650
And those are two
very distinct steps.

00:29:41.650 --> 00:29:45.760
I think it's inductive
versus deductive.

00:29:45.760 --> 00:29:48.880
And in a very similar
way, neural networks

00:29:48.880 --> 00:29:51.460
and developing neural networks
have the same problem,

00:29:51.460 --> 00:29:55.390
where we can't directly
point at a line of code

00:29:55.390 --> 00:29:59.290
for why a model makes
a decision it does.

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:02.380
But, instead, you
created code that

00:30:02.380 --> 00:30:04.990
made that model,
which means more

00:30:04.990 --> 00:30:10.270
of the top-down
type of debugging.

00:30:10.270 --> 00:30:12.550
You still need to be able
to look at specific code

00:30:12.550 --> 00:30:14.180
and try and figure
out what's going on.

00:30:14.180 --> 00:30:15.617
But in this way,
the reinforcement

00:30:15.617 --> 00:30:17.200
learning systems
really resemble a lot

00:30:17.200 --> 00:30:18.760
of the distributed systems.

00:30:18.760 --> 00:30:21.100
And the idea that
debugging is a process

00:30:21.100 --> 00:30:24.100
of generating
hypotheses, figuring out

00:30:24.100 --> 00:30:27.490
how to test the hypothesis
in isolation, verifying.

00:30:27.490 --> 00:30:29.995
OK, well, I did this,
I expected this,

00:30:29.995 --> 00:30:33.040
this other thing happened-- what
does that mean for my intuition

00:30:33.040 --> 00:30:35.050
about how these
systems are working?

00:30:35.050 --> 00:30:39.790
All right, so let's sort
of wrap things up a bit.

00:30:39.790 --> 00:30:44.350
There's two major
parts of this--

00:30:44.350 --> 00:30:47.530
the machine learning part and
the software engineering part.

00:30:47.530 --> 00:30:51.070
And I'm trying to focus on this
talk mostly on the how part.

00:30:51.070 --> 00:30:53.860
How did we get to 90 petaflops?

00:30:53.860 --> 00:30:55.157
How did we monitor health?

00:30:55.157 --> 00:30:56.990
How did we get the
performance that we need?

00:30:56.990 --> 00:30:58.600
How do we make it work?

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:00.141
I haven't touched,
basically, any

00:31:00.141 --> 00:31:01.390
of the machine learning parts.

00:31:01.390 --> 00:31:03.740
Why is the network this way?

00:31:03.740 --> 00:31:05.410
Why are these
constants like this?

00:31:05.410 --> 00:31:08.110
Why is the network
structured like this?

00:31:08.110 --> 00:31:11.440
Those "why" questions,
those are pretty tough,

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:13.870
but we can't really
answer those whys until we

00:31:13.870 --> 00:31:16.116
have the hows nailed down.

00:31:16.116 --> 00:31:18.490
So if you have your neural
network, your machine learning

00:31:18.490 --> 00:31:20.440
problems that you're
trying to figure out,

00:31:20.440 --> 00:31:23.050
the answers to these
questions, obviously, those

00:31:23.050 --> 00:31:24.830
are going to be domain specific.

00:31:24.830 --> 00:31:28.090
But when you say, OK, well, how
am I going to deploy and test

00:31:28.090 --> 00:31:31.470
these applications, how am I
going to test my hypotheses,

00:31:31.470 --> 00:31:33.810
the answer there has
been a cloud platform

00:31:33.810 --> 00:31:35.374
that's worked fantastically.

00:31:35.374 --> 00:31:38.040
So let's talk a little bit about
the things that did work really

00:31:38.040 --> 00:31:38.539
well.

00:31:38.539 --> 00:31:40.530
So I've included a little
Stackdriver dashboard

00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:41.430
that we used.

00:31:41.430 --> 00:31:42.810
Stackdriver was fantastic.

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:45.190
We could just throw
things to standard output

00:31:45.190 --> 00:31:48.390
and regex through them
without any trouble.

00:31:48.390 --> 00:31:52.170
I mentioned Cloud Storage also
scaling way beyond the level

00:31:52.170 --> 00:31:53.570
that we talked.

00:31:53.570 --> 00:31:56.850
So our early clusters were
putting out maybe 50,000 games

00:31:56.850 --> 00:31:59.460
a day with a full size model.

00:31:59.460 --> 00:32:02.880
With TPUs, we're putting out
over a million games a day

00:32:02.880 --> 00:32:06.630
and still having to download
and handle them all.

00:32:06.630 --> 00:32:08.582
And Cloud Storage has
just been a champ.

00:32:08.582 --> 00:32:10.790
At some point, we're thinking
about doing integration

00:32:10.790 --> 00:32:13.230
with Cloud Bigtable,
but that's still TBD.

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:16.090
And in the meantime, Cloud
Storage has just been--

00:32:16.090 --> 00:32:17.910
we just abuse it.

00:32:17.910 --> 00:32:19.709
It's really wonderful.

00:32:19.709 --> 00:32:21.750
Kubernetes Engine and
Cloud TPUs, as I mentioned,

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:23.333
those basically
worked out of the box.

00:32:23.333 --> 00:32:25.030
If you're interested
in using them,

00:32:25.030 --> 00:32:27.330
it's a pretty great
experience-- definitely sign up,

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:28.350
what you need to do.

00:32:28.350 --> 00:32:33.430
TensorBoard 1.9-- the latest
release of TensorBoard

00:32:33.430 --> 00:32:36.090
will let you serve your
TensorBoard dashboards right

00:32:36.090 --> 00:32:37.770
out of Cloud Storage,
which is great.

00:32:37.770 --> 00:32:39.570
If you are trying to
use the Cloud TPUs,

00:32:39.570 --> 00:32:42.420
the profiling tools
are easy to use.

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:44.970
They're useful to
dig into and find out

00:32:44.970 --> 00:32:48.120
how you can get the most
performance out of those TPUs.

00:32:48.120 --> 00:32:52.530
So all of these parts
of using the cloud stack

00:32:52.530 --> 00:32:53.970
went really well.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:33:00.750
As for those whys, I love this
quote here from Alex Ipran.

00:33:00.750 --> 00:33:03.370
Reinforcement learning
is really hard.

00:33:03.370 --> 00:33:04.730
He has this quote--

00:33:04.730 --> 00:33:08.790
if it turns out random, I have
no idea if it's something I did

00:33:08.790 --> 00:33:10.577
or if I just got unlucky.

00:33:10.577 --> 00:33:12.910
We have some of these algorithms
in the state of the art

00:33:12.910 --> 00:33:17.550
machine learning right now
that may be a correctly working

00:33:17.550 --> 00:33:20.250
version with a different
random seed will not

00:33:20.250 --> 00:33:23.677
converge on a solution, and
that's kind of astonishing.

00:33:23.677 --> 00:33:25.260
That here's a
successful result that's

00:33:25.260 --> 00:33:29.220
been published that only
works 30% of the time.

00:33:29.220 --> 00:33:32.850
Obviously, AlphaGo Zero
seems to be much more robust,

00:33:32.850 --> 00:33:35.940
but it kind of points
out the challenges

00:33:35.940 --> 00:33:38.640
in testing these
hypotheses, and the need

00:33:38.640 --> 00:33:42.690
to be able to iterate
and isolate and sort

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:47.080
of formulate hypotheses
and see if they work.

00:33:47.080 --> 00:33:51.550
This is kind of an amazing
quote that I really

00:33:51.550 --> 00:33:53.910
like to think about.

00:33:53.910 --> 00:33:56.070
For me personally,
I've been following

00:33:56.070 --> 00:33:57.600
in the footsteps
of published papers

00:33:57.600 --> 00:34:01.200
where I know what is
possible, because DeepMind

00:34:01.200 --> 00:34:03.450
had published their papers.

00:34:03.450 --> 00:34:05.670
It would be much
harder for me to--

00:34:05.670 --> 00:34:07.170
like, just trying
to imagine if I

00:34:07.170 --> 00:34:10.199
was in their shoes, where I get
a model that turns out pretty

00:34:10.199 --> 00:34:12.900
good, but not great.

00:34:12.900 --> 00:34:14.730
And I say to
myself, well, can it

00:34:14.730 --> 00:34:18.510
be better or is this the best
I can do with this approach?

00:34:18.510 --> 00:34:21.060
There's a very big difference
between exploring the unknown

00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:22.679
and reproducing the known.

00:34:22.679 --> 00:34:24.989
In my case, if I
hit a ceiling, I

00:34:24.989 --> 00:34:27.480
can compare that ceiling to
the ceiling that they described

00:34:27.480 --> 00:34:30.270
and say, OK, well, I
clearly have a bug.

00:34:30.270 --> 00:34:32.969
Whereas, for somebody doing new
research for the first time,

00:34:32.969 --> 00:34:36.480
it's going to be much harder for
you to say is this the ceiling

00:34:36.480 --> 00:34:38.699
or do I have bugs?

00:34:38.699 --> 00:34:42.659
You really need to be able
to be diligent and controlled

00:34:42.659 --> 00:34:44.647
and make sure that you
are able to isolate

00:34:44.647 --> 00:34:46.980
the parts of your system and
make sure that each of them

00:34:46.980 --> 00:34:47.980
is doing what you think.

00:34:47.980 --> 00:34:49.530
Does that kind of makes sense?

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:51.239
Great, awesome.

00:34:51.239 --> 00:34:54.330
And lastly, I kind
of want to quote

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.550
about why I'm really
excited as a Go player

00:34:56.550 --> 00:34:59.430
that all of these things
are finally taking shape.

00:34:59.430 --> 00:35:02.040
I started Minigo after
the AlphaGo paper

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:05.770
was published, which I think
was November of last year.

00:35:05.770 --> 00:35:09.171
And since then, Facebook
has announced an open source

00:35:09.171 --> 00:35:09.670
version.

00:35:09.670 --> 00:35:12.690
They've just released
a model Tencent.

00:35:12.690 --> 00:35:14.817
And other Chinese
companies have been

00:35:14.817 --> 00:35:17.400
working on models, which they've
released with various degrees

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:19.920
of openness.

00:35:19.920 --> 00:35:23.490
And it's been very
exciting to have these.

00:35:23.490 --> 00:35:26.796
And an open source project
called Leela Zero has also

00:35:26.796 --> 00:35:28.920
been done, where they've
been trying to crowdsource

00:35:28.920 --> 00:35:31.530
all of the GPU compute needed.

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:34.290
And it's been really
excellent to have

00:35:34.290 --> 00:35:36.840
all of these different
folks try to reproduce

00:35:36.840 --> 00:35:39.270
the paper with varying
amounts of success.

00:35:39.270 --> 00:35:41.220
And it's really
wonderful as a Go player

00:35:41.220 --> 00:35:45.280
to have access to these,
essentially, oracles.

00:35:45.280 --> 00:35:47.280
Go players like to think
of playing a game of Go

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:49.830
as having a conversation,
talking with someone.

00:35:49.830 --> 00:35:53.160
We have a great proverb
that playing a game of Go

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:56.020
with someone is like living
with them for a year.

00:35:56.020 --> 00:35:58.890
And in that case, we
have this new thing

00:35:58.890 --> 00:36:02.612
that is saying new
creative ideas to us

00:36:02.612 --> 00:36:04.320
that we haven't really
understood before.

00:36:04.320 --> 00:36:07.050
So if you are interested
in learning more

00:36:07.050 --> 00:36:09.610
about the game of Go,
definitely check it out online.

00:36:09.610 --> 00:36:10.994
There's a lot more resources.

00:36:10.994 --> 00:36:13.410
And, hopefully, it's going to
be a lot easier to learn now

00:36:13.410 --> 00:36:17.460
that we have ways
to understand it.

00:36:17.460 --> 00:36:20.700
We do try to hang stories
on our moves that we play.

00:36:20.700 --> 00:36:24.199
And it's going to be a little
bit easier for us to sort of do

00:36:24.199 --> 00:36:26.740
that and understand that as we
have better tools to dig into.

00:36:26.740 --> 00:36:28.830
So thank you all very much.

00:36:28.830 --> 00:36:32.910
A big thank you to the folks who
have helped donate their time

00:36:32.910 --> 00:36:35.070
to work on Minigo.

00:36:35.070 --> 00:36:37.440
That's Tom, Seth,
Brian, and Josh--

00:36:37.440 --> 00:36:40.470
they all have been instrumental
in making Minigo possible.

00:36:40.470 --> 00:36:43.520
[MUSIC PLAYING]

